00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:36.717
&gt;&gt; 11.

00:03:36.717 --> 00:03:36.778
[LAUGH].

00:03:36.778 --> 00:03:38.636
&gt;&gt; You can figure out.

00:03:38.636 --> 00:03:39.580
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:40.739
All right, thank you.

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:41.700
Zila.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:47.550
&gt;&gt; So I'm Nazila Alasti and I started life
as an engineer.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.593
I'm Iranian originally, and in my country
if you're a

00:03:50.593 --> 00:03:53.636
good student you either become a doctor or
an engineer,

00:03:53.636 --> 00:03:55.900
so there was, that was the choice and I
was

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.230
scared of blood so off I went into
engineering school.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:04.860
But it was actually a really great
background I thought for Silicon Valley.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.310
I didn't know I would end up here, but I
did.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:12.414
And I was thankful for my parents for
having pushed me into engineering.

00:04:12.414 --> 00:04:19.040
Fast forward, five years of working, at a
technology company at Mass Microdevices.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.000
I became a project designer, project lead,
went to business school,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.670
and learned that people can actually, make
money selling pencils.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.640
And that it doesn't have to be
semi-conductors.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.460
That was my big, learning from, from
business school.

00:04:35.512 --> 00:04:41.253
Entrepreneurial activities at the time
were not as hot as they are now.

00:04:41.253 --> 00:04:42.316
&gt;&gt; What was it, when did you graduate?

00:04:42.316 --> 00:04:43.839
&gt;&gt; I graduated in 88.

00:04:43.839 --> 00:04:44.470
&gt;&gt; 88.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:49.480
&gt;&gt; So, I also stopped aging at 30, a few
years earlier than you, yeah.

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:57.945
and, and, I have to say that my experience
at business school, was really

00:04:57.945 --> 00:05:01.360
eye-opening, broadening, but I wouldn't
say that

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.600
I was focused on necessarily becoming an
entrepreneur.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.238
It seemed very risky at the time.

00:05:07.238 --> 00:05:11.851
However after I got out and experienced
the venture capital world for

00:05:11.851 --> 00:05:15.648
a couple of years and then went on to work
at Apple, which

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:19.668
I consider really my formative years at
Apple, and worked on a

00:05:19.668 --> 00:05:25.962
big failure of a project called Newton,
which was the original hand-held device.

00:05:25.962 --> 00:05:29.986
I came to understand that I really needed
more freedom in my life,

00:05:29.986 --> 00:05:35.180
and that the corporate structure wasn't
providing that, and I was stupid enough or

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.543
naive enough to say that I could do things
on my own, so I

00:05:38.543 --> 00:05:44.188
started a long line of all sorts of
startups, failures as well as successes.

00:05:44.188 --> 00:05:50.895
And ended up now running Jooners, which
was my first, from Powerpoint, to funding,

00:05:50.895 --> 00:05:57.290
to product startup, where I'm CEO, founder
and I'm growing that business.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.668
So, that's a little bit about me.

00:05:58.668 --> 00:06:00.800
&gt;&gt; Zila's also the mother of two
daughters.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:02.170
&gt;&gt; Yes, I am.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:06.316
&gt;&gt; And, someone who is passionate about
trying to help those

00:06:06.316 --> 00:06:10.870
of you who are thinking about
entrepreneurship learn more about it.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.990
So that's why she's here today.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.308
&gt;&gt; Yeah, let me add to that Chuck.

00:06:14.308 --> 00:06:15.540
Thank you for bringing it up.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.781
I also want to talk to the women

00:06:18.781 --> 00:06:22.508
in, in the group if you're interested
afterwards about

00:06:22.508 --> 00:06:26.159
why I believe entrepreneurship is actually
a very valid

00:06:26.159 --> 00:06:30.790
choice and, and, and compatible with being
a mother.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:34.960
So anyone who is interested in discussing
that, I'm open to doing that.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.960
the world will tell you no, it's 24/7,
etc.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:41.580
Unless you burn the midnight oil, you
won't be successful.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.390
I happen to have a different story.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:45.599
So if anyone is interested I'm happy to
share that.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:47.000
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:47.680
Thank you.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.684
So we, we talked to, we've heard

00:06:49.684 --> 00:06:53.080
from two people who actually had technical
backgrounds.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:57.080
Will, give us your background and what
you're doing now.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:57.720
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Chuck.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:02.290
So I graduated in 1999 from Kellogg, and
I've been in the valley since then.

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:06.508
I've done a combination of both startups
and venture capital activity.

00:07:06.508 --> 00:07:08.203
So currently I am the CEO of

00:07:08.203 --> 00:07:12.110
Sequoia Hummer-Winblad funded company
called Widget Box.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:14.310
And I joined Widget Box two years ago.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:15.923
And before that, I was a managing director
at

00:07:15.923 --> 00:07:18.580
Hummer-Winblad and spent six years in the
venture capital business.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:21.337
So since I got out of business school,
I've

00:07:21.337 --> 00:07:25.585
had kind of the opportunity to see both,
entrepreneurs in

00:07:25.585 --> 00:07:29.759
action as a venture backer, and thinking
through business models

00:07:29.759 --> 00:07:33.690
and deciding who to finance and who not to
finance.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.730
And then also, having decided that to
become an entrepreneur myself

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:42.980
and it's only recently I even considered
myself one, quite honestly.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.059
But the last couple years of running a
