you know, how

00:08:01.191 --> 00:08:04.343
to, to lead a company, bring products to
market, sell, etc.

00:08:04.343 --> 00:08:07.721
And Chuck's right, like in terms of
backgrounds, I went to Harvard,

00:08:07.721 --> 00:08:11.513
studied East Asian studies and finance and
started my career actually in Asia.

00:08:11.513 --> 00:08:14.757
So I worked in Hong Kong, and Singapore,
spoke Mandarin

00:08:14.757 --> 00:08:17.191
Chinese and thought I was going to be part
of

00:08:17.191 --> 00:08:20.975
the Asian miracle, and, but so that, so
I've done

00:08:20.975 --> 00:08:24.790
I'm not coming from an engineering
background, in other words.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.580
And so part of, you know, one of the
questions today I

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:30.184
think is, how do MBAs and finance people
fit in to a

00:08:30.184 --> 00:08:34.524
culture that's really run by, by
engineers, as opposed to business types,

00:08:34.524 --> 00:08:38.110
and kinda the MBA being a joke, as opposed
to something good.

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:42.898
So I, you know, I'm happy to be here and
look forward to a good discussion.

00:08:42.898 --> 00:08:48.063
&gt;&gt; Now one of the things, Will, if I am
correct in reading this and listening to

00:08:48.063 --> 00:08:53.570
you, you formed, you joined Widget Box
after it had actually been founded.

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:54.020
&gt;&gt; That's correct.

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:56.090
So there were three technical founders.

00:08:56.090 --> 00:08:58.531
They'd actually raised $10 million before
I got there.

00:08:58.531 --> 00:08:59.750
&gt;&gt; Some from you.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:03.241
Some for me, I was the seed financier and
then Sequoia did the A

00:09:03.241 --> 00:09:07.694
round and then Brent Jones and Tommy
Vardell at Northgate did the B round.

00:09:07.694 --> 00:09:10.807
And at that point they were looking for a
CEO and

00:09:10.807 --> 00:09:13.525
the, they did a nine month search and the
last guy

00:09:13.525 --> 00:09:16.042
that we made the offer to, I told myself
at the

00:09:16.042 --> 00:09:20.190
dinner if this guy doesn't take the job,
I'm doing it.

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:20.772
He didn't take the job.

00:09:20.772 --> 00:09:23.435
[LAUGH] So, yeah.

00:09:23.435 --> 00:09:25.962
&gt;&gt; So, so, so some different paths here, I

00:09:25.962 --> 00:09:29.290
mean starting their own company, going out
and trying

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:32.253
to find the idea, put the team together,
versus

00:09:32.253 --> 00:09:35.750
joining a startup after it's had the
initial founders.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.105
The way we want to start this is to, is to
ask them

00:09:40.105 --> 00:09:45.034
a little bit about what led them to decide
to follow an entrepreneurial path.

00:09:45.034 --> 00:09:47.904
Because many of you are in business
school, or

00:09:47.904 --> 00:09:51.952
not in business school, you're thinking
about this and you've

00:09:51.952 --> 00:09:54.011
got a set of ideas in your head as to

00:09:54.011 --> 00:09:57.120
what it's going to be like to be an
entrepreneur.

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.480
And then, what has been the biggest
surprise

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.830
for them as they joined the
entrepreneurial world?

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.853
So you got a vision, what led you to, to,
to do this,

00:10:08.853 --> 00:10:13.510
and then, what's been the biggest surprise
once you, once you jumped in?

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:14.280
Let's start with Nazila.

00:10:16.046 --> 00:10:19.892
&gt;&gt; So I, I, I think I touched on that.

00:10:19.892 --> 00:10:22.625
For me, the, my job was such a big part

00:10:22.625 --> 00:10:25.958
of my life that, unless I was very
creative and

00:10:25.958 --> 00:10:28.863
had the freedom to do what I wanted to do,

00:10:28.863 --> 00:10:34.090
didn't have to explain myself too much, I
was unhappy.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:35.340
So I thought that a smaller

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:38.940
environment, less bureaucratic environment
would afford that.

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:43.025
I wanted to be my own boss, and, kind

00:10:43.025 --> 00:10:47.590
of live and die by the decisions that, I
made.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:51.014
I also like adrenaline, I like living on
the edge.

00:10:51.014 --> 00:10:53.634
I like being pushed to make decisions.

00:10:53.634 --> 00:10:56.344
I, felt that I had too much cushion at

00:10:56.344 --> 00:11:01.530
Apple, frankly, my decisions really didn't
make a difference.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.860
The juggernaut was going on, and I was
doing my best

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:10.260
but, you know, er, wh, wh, where was my
contribution really.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:12.684
So those were some of the reasons that

00:11:12.684 --> 00:11:16.170
I decided to jump into the entrepreneurial
pool.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:21.350
Er, the biggest surprise though, and this,
probably you all are smarter than I was.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.560
I, I'm an immigrant so I always thought of
myself as someone who

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:28.200
can do anything, just, just, give the
problem to me, I'll do it.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:29.590
I'll take care of it.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:32.293
I really never put too much emphasis or

00:11:32.293 --> 00:11:35.448
attention on partners and I learned the
lesson the

00:11:35.448 --> 00:11:39.352
hard way, that in the startup environment,
the biggest

00:11:39.352 --> 00:11:43.150
surprise for me was how much you needed
partners.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:46.406
Just like you can't have a, well you can
have a baby on your own I

00:11:46.406 --> 00:11:48.862
suppose, you can adopt one, and maybe
that's

00:11:48.862 --> 00:11:50.840
what you did, coming in late as a CEO.

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:51.480
I don't know.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:53.766
The analogy stops there.

00:11:53.766 --> 00:11:59.860
but, but, but, you know, I cannot
emphasize the importance of partners.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:04.010
And I cannot emphasize how much I was
lacking in that dimension.

00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:07.572
And how much I wish, had I done things a
different way,

00:12:07.572 --> 00:12:11.960
to go back and make ideas, joint ideas as
opposed to my idea.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:13.264
So that was the biggest surprise for me.

00:12:13.264 --> 00:12:17.393
&gt;&gt; You thinking about a, a partner, is
this a founding partner, or you thinking

00:12:17.393 --> 00:12:19.395
about a partner in terms of somebody in

00:12:19.395 --> 00:12:21.918
the supply chain or the distribution chain
or?

00:12:21.918 --> 00:12:25.050
&gt;&gt; Oh, the founding partner more.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:30.790
Yeah, and I also, just let me briefly say
that I think, my

00:12:30.790 --> 00:12:35.859
experience in business school told me what
I was good at, not was I was lacking.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:40.603
And, so I, if I were to do it again, I
would emphasize a lot

00:12:40.603 --> 00:12:45.320
what I'm lacking, so that that defines the
type of partner you'll be looking for.

00:12:46.546 --> 00:12:49.915
So more on that if the discussion goes
that way.

00:12:49.915 --> 00:12:50.430
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:50.930
Will?

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:53.204
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for me I think it was

00:12:53.204 --> 00:12:55.760
just, a combination of a lot of different
variables.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:58.194
But if you, if you think about where we
live today, right

00:12:58.194 --> 00:13:01.141
now, in this valley, it's almost like how
could you not do it.

00:13:01.141 --> 00:13:02.730
You know, if not now, when, you know.

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:04.860
And this is, the whole ecosystem here

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:08.790
is geared toward risk taking, testing
yourself.

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:09.990
There's a whole, you know, obviously, a

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:12.170
huge base of institutional venture capital
support.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:13.960
Legal systems are here.

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.768
And it, really it came down to, I remember
to doing investment when

00:13:16.768 --> 00:13:19.750
I was in the, in the investment side about
how to test semiconductors.

00:13:19.750 --> 00:13:22.215
And at the time there was, the
state-of-the-art

00:13:22.215 --> 00:13:24.063
was testing a quarter of the wafer, and

00:13:24.063 --> 00:13:25.743
then it was half the wafer, and then

00:13:25.743 --> 00:13:29.120
finally this breakthrough to test the full
wafer.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:31.800
And I feel like entrepreneurship is a full
wafer test.

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:34.240
It's every, testing every part of who you
are.

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:39.446
It's testing your passion, your endurance,
your perseverance, your leadership

00:13:39.446 --> 00:13:44.860
skills, your sales ability, you know, it,
it, it, and I just felt that if I was

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:49.197
65 looking back on my career, I would
never forgive myself for living

00:13:49.197 --> 00:13:54.010
in this environment, at this time and not
testing the whole wafer, you know.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:56.190
And so, venture capital is a great job

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.870
but it's, it's literally it's it's a
research job.

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:03.780
You know, where, and, and it's a job where
you make investment decisions.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:07.327
And I always felt when I met with the
entrepreneurs, I'm like, God, these guys.

00:14:07.327 --> 00:14:09.767
You know, I admire them so much, and it
just got

00:14:09.767 --> 00:14:12.380
to the point where I, I couldn't imagine
not doing it.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:16.640
&gt;&gt; And once you jumped in, what was the
biggest surprise?

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:23.523
&gt;&gt; The biggest surprise was, it being okay
that you don't have all

00:14:23.523 --> 00:14:30.370
the answers, and trusting in process to
help you get to an answer.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:33.200
Like I really thought, cuz as an investor
you're hypothesis driven.

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:36.461
You believe that you're making an
investment based on

00:14:36.461 --> 00:14:39.770
the following characterization, a, b and
c, therefore, d.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:45.302
And as soon as you start running a company
you realize none of those hypothesis

00:14:45.302 --> 00:14:50.340
work, and because the venture guy, you're
paid to have an opinion.

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:53.698
When they say, Will, what's your opinion
on this deal uou have an opinion.

00:14:53.698 --> 00:14:57.185
And all the sudden I realized, like, shit,
I don't even know what to do now.

00:14:57.185 --> 00:15:00.788
And realizing that that was a very
unnatural feeling because I.

00:15:00.788 --> 00:15:05.222
I'd find in myself of always knowing of
you and realizing now that I don't know.

00:15:05.222 --> 00:15:07.292
And we've basically, in the context of
this is

00:15:07.292 --> 00:15:09.980
we had no idea how to make money, none.

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:13.437
And like dealing with that and accepting
that and then realizing,

00:15:13.437 --> 00:15:16.760
like, well how are we gonna get out of
this problem?

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:19.010
And just deciding that, okay, this, I'm
reminded

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:20.860
of when Nick Saven won the national chair
and

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:22.510
he said that hey, we believe in process

00:15:22.510 --> 00:15:24.460
not results at Alabama and what did that
mean.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.600
What he meant was that if you know how to
block,

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.120
you how to play defense, you know how to
do these

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:30.960
things, you just play the game and then
you trust the

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.330
process to help you get to the result that
you want.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.193
But, so rather than a random act of

00:15:35.193 --> 00:15:37.703
heroism where you're gonna have this flash
of

00:15:37.703 --> 00:15:40.469
genius that's gonna make you rich, just
deciding

00:15:40.469 --> 00:15:42.978
slowly steadily plod your way in a
predictable

00:15:42.978 --> 00:15:45.488
way where you're going and then trusting
that

00:15:45.488 --> 00:15:47.740
that's gonna have you arrive at the right

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.669
answer as opposed to a prior saying I

00:15:49.669 --> 00:15:52.550
have the answer, now we'll just make that
happen.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:55.295
That was a really hard transition from you
know being

00:15:55.295 --> 00:15:59.025
an investment hypothesis driven thinker to
being a process driven thinker.

00:15:59.025 --> 00:15:59.325
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:15:59.325 --> 00:16:03.387
&gt;&gt; MIke [COUGH] out, out of engineering
school I

00:16:03.387 --> 00:16:08.450
went and worked at place Hughes, Hughes
Aircraft Company.

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:11.950
And one of the projects I got to work on
wa a solar powered race car.

00:16:11.950 --> 00:16:14.260
And it was a totally cool job.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:18.290
We raced across Australia from Darwin to
Adelaide.

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:20.802
And this car, before we built this car,

00:16:20.802 --> 00:16:23.448
the fastest, again, this was a long time
ago,

00:16:23.448 --> 00:16:26.097
the fastest solar powered cars were going
maybe

00:16:26.097 --> 00:16:28.700
20 miles an hour, maybe 22 miles an hour.

00:16:29.704 --> 00:16:35.770
and, we built a car that could go 55 miles
an hour forever, just on sunlight.

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:39.730
Which is, from a knee jerk perspective
like it's an amazing accomplishment.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.570
And we won the race and we came back and

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:46.940
I remember clear as ever my boss saying
that's amazing.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.115
You know, I just wanna let you know the
average race

00:16:49.115 --> 00:16:52.375
at Hughes Aircraft Company is 6%, you're
gonna get 6.5% this year.

00:16:52.375 --> 00:16:56.734
[LAUGH] And just the feeling at that
moment of you know, your fate

00:16:56.734 --> 00:17:00.856
being in your own hands was like okay this
is not gonna work.

00:17:00.856 --> 00:17:06.746
[LAUGH] I was also fired between, between
first and second year at business school.

00:17:06.746 --> 00:17:08.270
I went to work with a consultant company.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:12.860
At the end of the summer everyone got an
offer to come back except for me.

00:17:12.860 --> 00:17:16.060
And they fired me because I scowled at
people senior to me in the firm.

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:20.185
And they, they told me that I, I admired
them for at least telling me that,

00:17:20.185 --> 00:17:22.542
but you know, talk about wanting to be

00:17:22.542 --> 00:17:26.106
in control, these are all, sort of, strong
influences.

00:17:26.106 --> 00:17:29.385
[LAUGH] and, and one other thing, sorta,
how you get into

00:17:29.385 --> 00:17:34.163
entrepreneurship I, I was I was
interviewed on Japanese TV actually

00:17:34.163 --> 00:17:36.621
and I said, well I think one of the key
tests

00:17:36.621 --> 00:17:39.980
is how do you feel at 9 o'clock on Sunday
night.

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:43.780
If you feel like oh, you know yeah, it was
a great weekend.

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:46.836
I'm looking forward to this week cuz of
cool projects, that's one thing,

00:17:46.836 --> 00:17:49.620
but if you feel like oh, man, I can't
believe the weekend's over.

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:52.130
I'm really not looking for work, it's time
to change.

00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:54.537
Well they show this on Japanese TV and
actually showed

00:17:54.537 --> 00:17:56.997
this at 9 o'clock on Sunday night and
apparently they

00:17:56.997 --> 00:18:00.151
got flooded with 12,000 phone calls to the
switchboards or

00:18:00.151 --> 00:18:02.528
whatever saying, yes, we, I need to get a
new job.

00:18:02.528 --> 00:18:08.110
[LAUGH] So so, so, so, so those were my
influences doing a new start up

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:13.614
and the biggest surprise for me at the
risk of Chuck's wrath because this

00:18:13.614 --> 00:18:19.370
is one of the Ben said things yeah, in
terms of, I believe the most important

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:24.740
things are a small team with high morale
is really hard to defeat.

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:29.380
And you can, we had to change, three out
of my four companies had to change.

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:31.480
Original idea completely failed and had to
change to

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:33.530
a new idea and that was a surprise to me.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:36.832
It wasn't so much the quality of the
original idea, but it was the quality

00:18:36.832 --> 00:18:40.100
of the team and the morale of the team
that I think we're, the key think.

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:42.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah, interesting, so yeah.

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:48.370
Interesting so, similar to the point, is
that you need a team, you need a high

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:55.970
performance team, because let me just
generalize what you just said Mike.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:01.080
There are very few companies that start
off maybe here, and just go like that.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:04.890
Most of them go, like this, even if they
get there,

00:19:04.890 --> 00:19:07.310
and many more go like this, and end up
over here.

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:08.250
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:10.323
&gt;&gt; And, the question is, how do you, how
do

00:19:10.323 --> 00:19:14.160
you do, this, sometimes what's called the
art of small-boat sailing.

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:17.720
Any of you who're sailors, you know, you
tack back and forth.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:19.875
And a good team is the, is the way to do
it.

00:19:19.875 --> 00:19:20.375
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:25.028
&gt;&gt; So, so now we're gonna, we're gonna
sort

00:19:25.028 --> 00:19:27.600
of turn a little bit to thank you very
much.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:30.970
We're gonna turn a little bit to lessons
learned.

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:35.293
And, and to start off just asking each one
of the panelists so

00:19:35.293 --> 00:19:40.007
what what are the two key lessons, one of
two lessons that have

00:19:40.007 --> 00:19:45.192
been important to your success, lack of
success, you know, if there'd been

00:19:45.192 --> 00:19:48.100
failures, you know, you, you, you learn

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:51.609
more from failures often than from
successes.

00:19:51.609 --> 00:19:56.119
And how and where did you learn it, and
then we're gonna,

00:19:56.119 --> 00:20:01.655
gonna, after we leave that, we'll come and
talk about reflecting back

00:20:01.655 --> 00:20:07.528
on the your business school experience and
to find out sort of,

00:20:07.528 --> 00:20:14.020
what you think was, was most important
about your business school experience.

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.140
So let's start with what are one or two
key lessons that

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:20.350
have been important to your success, lack
of success in your entrepreneurial career.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.629
How and where did you learn them, and,

00:20:22.629 --> 00:20:25.908
let's get started with let's, let's start
with Will.

00:20:25.908 --> 00:20:27.401
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:20:27.401 --> 00:20:32.526
[LAUGH] I, I would say [COUGH] the kinda,
I don't

00:20:32.526 --> 00:20:37.751
wanna repeat myself a little bit, but I
think what Mike said is

00:20:37.751 --> 00:20:43.178
also really important, too, like, you
know, Andy [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:43.178 --> 00:20:48.002
wrote the book Plan B, and they basically,
it's,

00:20:48.002 --> 00:20:53.270
it's the same point Chuck made about
tacking back and forth.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:59.653
It's, it's, it's being, having a smart,
high-energy team

00:20:59.653 --> 00:21:05.039
that learns really rapidly what the
signals the market are giving it,

00:21:05.039 --> 00:21:08.201
and you know, we, we have a thesis about
our

00:21:08.201 --> 00:21:12.850
business and now we're on like thesis four
I think, probably.

00:21:14.196 --> 00:21:18.973
And recognizing that like I use to
remember Chuck was on the board of ConEd

00:21:18.973 --> 00:21:21.188
and the founder of ConEd told me once,

00:21:21.188 --> 00:21:23.910
when I was thinking about starting a
company.

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:27.019
He said, hey listen the first step is the
hardest, but once you

00:21:27.019 --> 00:21:30.730
start [COUGH] your just on a journey and
process, and I think that;s right.

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:35.070
Like so beginning this work, be very, very
aggressive about it.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:38.563
But at the same time be very flexible as a
team about

00:21:38.563 --> 00:21:43.471
how to attack based on what you're hearing
so thesis around revenue or

00:21:43.471 --> 00:21:47.632
monozation or customer acquisition, market
segment

00:21:47.632 --> 00:21:50.874
positioning all that you it should be

00:21:50.874 --> 00:21:57.250
taken with, with a degree of flexibility
so you can re, reconstitute yourself.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:00.100
And then being able to work with investors
is really critical around that.

00:22:00.100 --> 00:22:02.710
Because investors again, going back to my
original

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:04.990
premise about difference between start ups
and investing.

00:22:04.990 --> 00:22:06.210
Investors are thesis driven.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:08.540
So if you say we are gonna go do this.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:12.430
I'm raising money for this purpose, and we
are gonna attack this market this way.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:14.240
We are gonna characterize revenue model
this way.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:17.228
And, the first board meeting you are like,
well,

00:22:17.228 --> 00:22:20.060
[LAUGH] that's not why it not work, you
know?

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:22.190
Having the ability to have that
conversation with them.

00:22:22.190 --> 00:22:28.670
So their with you through the journey as
opposed to being surprised by the attack.

00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:30.761
And so I would say being able to work down
with your

00:22:30.761 --> 00:22:33.158
team and try to attack the market and
learn and then other

00:22:33.158 --> 00:22:35.708
challenge is how do you work back up with
your investor group

00:22:35.708 --> 00:22:38.990
to basically say, okay, well, here's what
we're seeing in the market.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:40.290
Here's the data points.

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:41.650
Here's how I'd characterize those.

00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:42.780
Here's why I think we need to move

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:46.630
direction a little bit, and then, making
that an

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:49.710
open conversation, so they become part of
the

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:53.660
solution as opposed to like, critically
observing your problem.

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:55.600
So, I don't know if that makes any sense.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:23:01.330
&gt;&gt; So, so the lesson is, I'll be ready for
changes of course, and embrace them.

00:23:01.330 --> 00:23:01.830
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:05.489
&gt;&gt; And also, have the courage to go back
to your investor group and

00:23:05.489 --> 00:23:10.320
explain to them, this is not, this company
is not doing what they invested in.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:10.520
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:13.410
&gt;&gt; And, and your point, your point about
as an investor,

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:17.100
you've got a thesis, and you are investing
in a market.

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:20.343
And if at the first board meeting, they
come back and

00:23:20.343 --> 00:23:24.320
say, oops sorry, guys, I just took your
$10 million, but-.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:24.850
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:26.400
&gt;&gt; You're not going there.

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:26.570
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:23:26.570 --> 00:23:31.602
Another thing I've, I've, I really learn I
think, and I say this with all due

00:23:31.602 --> 00:23:37.730
respect is that investors are extremely
opinionated, like, you know, alpha male.

00:23:37.730 --> 00:23:40.240
Type people typically.

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:43.000
And they, and they have a lots of
opinions.

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:45.440
The other thing I learned was to

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:48.800
acknowledge and be respectful and listen
to all

00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:51.350
of them without being overly reactive
[LAUGH]

00:23:51.350 --> 00:23:54.210
to respond to any particular one of them.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:57.961
I got, what I mean by that, is I'm gonna
say you know, you should do this,

00:23:57.961 --> 00:24:02.150
this, this and this and I'd go back and
I'd go okay well that doesn't make sense.

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:03.700
Why would I do that?

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:07.100
And but, I, I was over, so I guess there's

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:09.800
a fine line between having an open
conversation with investors and

00:24:09.800 --> 00:24:12.600
being extremely respectful of there
opinions because CEOs that don't

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:16.050
listen to their investors get fired
relatively quickly in my experience.

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:19.510
So, you listen, but then you still are in
charge.

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:22.640
And so, you have to decide how you
incorporate

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:24.840
their feedback, make them feel like
they've been heard without

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:26.830
making decisions that you go against your
own gut

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:28.450
feel or what you think the business should
really do.

00:24:28.450 --> 00:24:30.940
That's something that's also hard to
learn, I think.

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:35.010
&gt;&gt; Just, so, you were, he was an investor
with a firm called Hummer Winblad.

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:39.070
One of those two was Ann Winblad.

00:24:39.070 --> 00:24:40.510
so, if we change a little bit the

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:43.420
alpha male to very determined and, and
opinionated.

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:43.810
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:24:43.810 --> 00:24:44.370
&gt;&gt; How is that?

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:45.700
&gt;&gt; That's very, very fair.

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:46.127
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:24:46.127 --> 00:24:46.627
Mike.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:52.840
&gt;&gt; So for me, one of the golden rules is
speed is the ultimate weapon.

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:57.910
And I've learned that in, in a good way
and a bad way.

00:24:57.910 --> 00:24:59.637
I have all these little sayings.

00:24:59.637 --> 00:25:03.020
[COUGH] I think that the probability that
any business development

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:06.600
deal ever happening declines by 10%
everyday it doesn't close.

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:09.950
And I really believe that.

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:13.760
so, you know, mi, we closed the Microsoft
deal with direct

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:17.670
hit where our results were shown on the
Microsoft search result page.

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:20.750
And that deal was done in, in less than
ten days.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:22.292
And I think if it stretched on to, you
know,

00:25:22.292 --> 00:25:24.520
six weeks or something it would have never
even gotten done.

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:26.912
Same with the AOL deal.

00:25:26.912 --> 00:25:30.410
I've just seen from my you know, history
which ones close and which ones don't.

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:32.820
Same thing with, with raising money.

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:36.564
I think I've raised seven rounds of
financing and six out

00:25:36.564 --> 00:25:41.044
of the seven had a signed term sheet the
day I pitched.

00:25:41.044 --> 00:25:46.111
And I think if we stretch it out longer,
all these questions and second thoughts,

00:25:46.111 --> 00:25:51.490
and more market research and whatever
comes up and, and even closing employees.

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:54.829
I, someone comes in for an interview,
we'll do

00:25:54.829 --> 00:25:59.285
do diligence checks while they're still
interviewing other people in

00:25:59.285 --> 00:26:03.221
the company, and before they leave the
building, we'll give

00:26:03.221 --> 00:26:06.650
them an offer and say, are you ready to
accept?

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:08.810
And they're like floored.

00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:09.171
And right now?

00:26:09.171 --> 00:26:09.945
[LAUGH] We say you know yeah.

00:26:09.945 --> 00:26:12.480
And we don't wanna rush you sleep and let
us know 9 o'clock tomorrow morning.

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.028
[LAUGH] But we get a lot of those people,
and if we don't,

00:26:15.028 --> 00:26:18.190
I've seen people spread out and then you,
you know, you lose them.

00:26:18.190 --> 00:26:21.512
Also, launching products, all my company
we've launched, launched a product within

00:26:21.512 --> 00:26:24.098
three months, three and a half months of,
of starting the company.

00:26:24.098 --> 00:26:26.188
And then you, it's not a very
full-featured product

00:26:26.188 --> 00:26:28.380
but you get the feedback from the market
and everything.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:33.070
So speed for me is far and away the, the
most important lesson I've learned.

00:26:33.070 --> 00:26:38.317
&gt;&gt; So, one of the points you made there
had to do with with raising

00:26:38.317 --> 00:26:43.730
money and, and signing the, the deal the
same day you, you pitched it.

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:44.310
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:26:44.310 --> 00:26:46.320
&gt;&gt; Were you able to do that the first time
out?

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:48.610
Let's assume that, that, that, that these
people

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:50.010
are all gonna be the first time out.

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:50.970
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:53.300
So, people always ask that.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:57.435
And so let me just say, part of the, part
of the, the, party

00:26:57.435 --> 00:27:01.820
of the reason all of us I think here are
doing this is for aspirations.

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:05.038
To sort of get you guys, you know,
motivated and excited so

00:27:05.038 --> 00:27:08.150
some of these things may seem like oh, I
can't believe that.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:10.970
But it's sort of a goal to go after.

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:14.453
But actually J for Jertson my second
company so

00:27:14.453 --> 00:27:17.540
I had one under my belt, $13 million one.

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:21.120
We pitched them at 8:15 on a Tuesday
morning in April of

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:25.040
1998, and we got a signed term sheet from
them by 4:30.

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:28.160
And the way you do that, anyone in this
room can do that.

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:30.535
I think some of the techniques are you
come into

00:27:30.535 --> 00:27:33.670
that meeting with what I call and if then
contract.

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:35.855
And if then contract I go get a customer
for

00:27:35.855 --> 00:27:38.930
the, for the product and, and make this
amazing claims.

00:27:38.930 --> 00:27:40.828
Like, if we can provide search results in

00:27:40.828 --> 00:27:43.107
50 milliseconds that 80% of your users
think

00:27:43.107 --> 00:27:45.549
our results better than your [UNKNOWN]
results, would

00:27:45.549 --> 00:27:47.570
you buy them for a dollar per thousand?

00:27:47.570 --> 00:27:50.020
And almost anyone will say yeah, okay.

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:51.981
So, they sign a letter or whatever and,
and

00:27:51.981 --> 00:27:55.300
then walk into the troops Trevor Fischer
with this letter.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.190
Now there's all sorts of caveats and
clauses and no legal contract, but

00:27:58.190 --> 00:28:01.110
at least Trevor Fischer says okay, you
know market risk has been eliminated.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:02.420
He's got a customer.

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:06.430
And also you walk into that meeting with
sort of how your gonna build that product.

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:07.740
Here's the Gantt chart, here's the people.

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:08.790
Here's the three people I'm gonna hire.

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:11.050
I've already talked to them, they've
already committed to joining.

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:12.622
Here's the, you now, the database schema.

00:28:12.622 --> 00:28:14.885
Trevor Fischer doesn't know whether the
scheme is right or

00:28:14.885 --> 00:28:16.840
not right, or which way they are going to
take six.

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:20.660
But at least they think, okay, this person
seems to know where they are going.

00:28:20.660 --> 00:28:22.900
And so, it is much easier for them to say
okay, yeah.

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:25.160
And of course you also play competitive
pressure.

00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:27.052
I always do my meetings, I'm meeting, you
know,

00:28:27.052 --> 00:28:29.630
Will's been on the receiving side of this
probably.

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:31.050
I meet with three VCs in the same day.

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:32.527
And I say it's moving fast, and I'm

00:28:32.527 --> 00:28:35.490
actually expecting a term sheet by 5
o'clock today.

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:38.290
And sometimes, they'll call me back the
next day and say, we're in.

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:38.950
And I say, it's gone.

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:40.530
And they're like, what do you mean it's
gone?

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:41.570
And I say, I told you 5 o'clock.

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:43.060
And they say, but all entrepreneurs say
that.

00:28:43.060 --> 00:28:44.550
And I say, well, sometimes, it is gone.

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:48.300
So I think if you just project this, if
you project this, you can get it done.

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:51.570
And a lot of times, you know, the venture
capitalists will sense this.

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:54.410
And they say, wow, this is, this is
moving.

00:28:54.410 --> 00:28:55.449
&gt;&gt; Who were you dealing with at Draper.

00:28:56.742 --> 00:28:57.680
&gt;&gt; On that day?

00:28:57.680 --> 00:28:58.100
&gt;&gt; Yea.

00:28:58.100 --> 00:29:03.638
&gt;&gt; I had Tim Steve and John all in the
room at 8:15.

00:29:03.638 --> 00:29:04.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:04.490 --> 00:29:06.814
They are a group that can and does,

00:29:06.814 --> 00:29:10.434
and do make quick decision, so that's
pretty good.

00:29:10.434 --> 00:29:11.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:29:13.064 --> 00:29:15.534
&gt;&gt; Let's see, who do we [UNKNOWN].

00:29:15.534 --> 00:29:16.162
&gt;&gt; Yeah wow.

00:29:16.162 --> 00:29:17.431
I'm floored.

00:29:17.431 --> 00:29:19.990
[LAUGH] I want you on my board.

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:23.960
My experience has been very different.

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:28.007
The small sailboat analogy I think
corresponds to our

00:29:28.007 --> 00:29:34.114
journey definitely with Juners the company
I'm leading right now.

00:29:34.114 --> 00:29:39.204
It was different with e-circles this was
late 90s, you

00:29:39.204 --> 00:29:44.103
know 98 99, we raised $27 million and it
was very

00:29:44.103 --> 00:29:48.423
much of this quick you know, now or never
but

00:29:48.423 --> 00:29:53.066
I guess my biggest lesson is that you
shouldn't give up.

00:29:53.066 --> 00:29:56.067
And if 9 o'clock at night on Sunday you
still feel that you

00:29:56.067 --> 00:30:01.108
have the passion, it's an it's, it's, it's
an affair that's not finished.

00:30:01.108 --> 00:30:03.300
If you're still coming up with good ideas.

00:30:03.300 --> 00:30:07.461
Then keep on at it, even if you don't have
the term sheet, right?

00:30:08.573 --> 00:30:13.770
so, so persistence, always pays.

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:17.110
And it's kind of what Will was saying
about the process.

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:20.359
Be very, focused on what you're doing,
where you

00:30:20.359 --> 00:30:22.860
are in the process, and don't give it up.

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:24.670
Don't let people derail you.

00:30:24.670 --> 00:30:25.570
Because they will try to.

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:31.280
The other lesson I learned is that, maybe
I'm too much of an engineer.

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:34.970
I, I really don't have it in me to go and
bluff like that.

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:35.745
&gt;&gt; Yes you do.

00:30:35.745 --> 00:30:35.984
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:30:35.984 --> 00:30:36.679
&gt;&gt; yeah, exactly.

00:30:36.679 --> 00:30:38.802
So I need you as a coach.

00:30:38.802 --> 00:30:44.580
I, I, I, I will urge all of you to find a
mic and learn from.

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:49.308
Seriously, there have been board members
in my company that have told

00:30:49.308 --> 00:30:53.040
me, you did this and this is how you spoke
about it?

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:58.420
I think it's also, perhaps, not too fine a
point on it, a female characteristic.

00:30:58.420 --> 00:31:01.390
Well, you know, yeah, you were expecting
me to do this, right?

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:03.270
Why should I even brag about it?

00:31:03.270 --> 00:31:06.370
As opposed to, because you're dealing most
of the time

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:09.750
with these alpha males, that are, I
characterize them as lions.

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:10.350
Right?

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:11.310
I am an ant.

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:12.845
You know, I build.

00:31:12.845 --> 00:31:17.633
[LAUGH] And then I face the lion, and the
lion just basically, all

00:31:17.633 --> 00:31:22.015
he does all day long is sit under the
shade [LAUGH] and watch.

00:31:22.015 --> 00:31:25.630
[LAUGH] Ooh, that looks like a good prey
over there.

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:26.020
Attack.

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:28.610
And that, when the lion is attacking Mike,
Mike

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:31.050
is like, whoa, I've got 3 other lions
coming.

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:32.010
Quick.

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:34.240
Whereas with me, I'm [LAUGH] okay.

00:31:34.240 --> 00:31:37.300
So we're laughing about this, but this is
a serious point.

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:41.276
Find out if you're an ant, get yourself a
lion partner

00:31:41.276 --> 00:31:47.050
somewhere, [LAUGH] if you're a lion, get
yourself an ant partner, right?

00:31:47.050 --> 00:31:51.651
Because both are necessary and that's the
combo is really unbeatable.

00:31:51.651 --> 00:31:55.020
The combo is what lets you do the speed.

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:55.387
Right?

00:31:55.387 --> 00:31:58.380
And the ant and the lion better trust each
other.

00:31:58.380 --> 00:31:59.580
You don't look like me.

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:02.240
[LAUGH] Yeah, yeah, yeah, I know you wear
lipstick.

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:02.710
Yeah, I know.

00:32:02.710 --> 00:32:06.590
But I have, I have a brain I can help, you
know.

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:08.850
Let me also share since I've got the

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:11.690
audience laughing, I think I have
permission here.

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:17.780
[LAUGH] I was 6 months pregnant with my
second daughter.

00:32:17.780 --> 00:32:20.610
Amazon made an offer for the company I was
at.

00:32:21.922 --> 00:32:26.340
E-Circles, at the time for $250 million.

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:28.260
$245 million to be very precise.

00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:32.860
Imagine, second child, belly comes that
much faster.

00:32:32.860 --> 00:32:36.389
I am this big, and I'm literally jumping
up and down on

00:32:36.389 --> 00:32:40.095
the table saying to the CEO, take it, take
it, take it.

00:32:40.095 --> 00:32:43.940
And he looks at me and he says, you're not
an entrepreneur.

00:32:43.940 --> 00:32:45.825
You really don't have the balls.

00:32:45.825 --> 00:32:47.990
[LAUGH] An entrepreneur will say no to
them.

00:32:47.990 --> 00:32:51.104
I'm like, no, this is $245 million.

00:32:51.104 --> 00:32:52.520
Don't say no.

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:55.871
We sold that company for $13 million to
classmates.com

00:32:55.871 --> 00:32:59.500
6 months from that point, right after, you
know, whatever.

00:32:59.500 --> 00:33:02.530
February of 2000, April of 2000, right?

00:33:02.530 --> 00:33:06.280
So at that point, I really felt this
small.

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:09.729
I'm like, oh, yeah, that's right, you
know, I'm pregnant and my

00:33:09.729 --> 00:33:13.710
hormones have gotten the better of me, and
I'm definitely not an entrepreneur.

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:17.400
That's why I'm working for this guy as a
VP of Marketing.

00:33:17.400 --> 00:33:18.600
I may have all these ideas.

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:22.080
I may know where I'm going, but yep, I'm
not an entrepreneur.

00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:25.748
Don't let ever the world tell you that
even if you're 6 months pregnant.

00:33:25.748 --> 00:33:27.697
[LAUGH] Right?

00:33:27.697 --> 00:33:29.370
Seriously.

00:33:29.370 --> 00:33:30.280
Stick to it.

00:33:30.280 --> 00:33:31.020
Go to the board.

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:31.830
Do whatever you need.

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:32.560
You know.

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:34.530
Because sometimes right.

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:37.420
People come in different packages.

00:33:37.420 --> 00:33:39.950
And don't let the world tell you that you
need to look different.

00:33:41.570 --> 00:33:43.270
anyway, I stop.

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:43.570
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:46.142
And there's a, there's a a female
intuition in

00:33:46.142 --> 00:33:48.840
some of these things I think that's pretty
important.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:49.790
I'll tell you one other story.

00:33:50.932 --> 00:33:55.855
Hotmail Samir Bartia had an offer from
Microsoft for $250 million.

00:33:55.855 --> 00:33:56.490
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:33:57.580 --> 00:33:58.460
&gt;&gt; Samir's from India.

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:03.403
Samir called home and he's talking to his
father and he said we just had an

00:34:03.403 --> 00:34:08.590
offer for $250 million from Microsoft and
his father says that is really great.

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:10.582
And he says I turned it down.

00:34:10.582 --> 00:34:12.255
[LAUGH] His father hangs up.

00:34:12.255 --> 00:34:13.340
[LAUGH].

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:16.732
&gt;&gt; A nanosecond later his mother's on the
phone saying, Samir,

00:34:16.732 --> 00:34:19.742
call that man back and tell him you'll
take that offer.

00:34:19.742 --> 00:34:21.215
[LAUGH] He took the offer.

00:34:21.215 --> 00:34:24.170
[LAUGH].

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:28.957
&gt;&gt; So the moral of the story is better be
a mother than an entrepreneur.

00:34:28.957 --> 00:34:30.281
[LAUGH] [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:30.281 --> 00:34:30.689
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:30.689 --> 00:34:31.400
Exactly.

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:35.060
&gt;&gt; And Steve Jurvetson was on the board
saying don't take the offer.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:35.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:35.270 --> 00:34:35.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:35.470 --> 00:34:35.970
Yep.

00:34:36.976 --> 00:34:42.180
&gt;&gt; well, so let's turn now to to Business
Sch, School education and

00:34:42.180 --> 00:34:47.848
how that might, or might not, have helped
prepare you for your work.

00:34:47.848 --> 00:34:52.111
In general, and in specific, instances,
sort of,

00:34:52.111 --> 00:34:55.860
in general, but then, they did or didn't.

00:34:55.860 --> 00:35:00.211
And then what were the most valuable
classes you took and what classes didn't

00:35:00.211 --> 00:35:04.250
you take that you wish you had taken and I
guess we'll start with Will.

00:35:04.250 --> 00:35:04.430
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:06.420
I've thought about this quite a bit.

00:35:06.420 --> 00:35:09.752
I guess I have 3 comments that I thought
of.

00:35:09.752 --> 00:35:11.620
This is more about what I didn't learn.

00:35:11.620 --> 00:35:13.970
The first is that risk is really relative.

00:35:13.970 --> 00:35:16.350
People think that doing start ups is
risky.

00:35:17.350 --> 00:35:18.755
My, a lot of the guys that when look

00:35:18.755 --> 00:35:21.580
back, I went to my tenth [UNKNOWN] this
past year.

00:35:21.580 --> 00:35:25.941
Went to Merrill Lynch, Morgan Stanley, BCG
and McKenzie.

00:35:25.941 --> 00:35:28.044
They all got fired.

00:35:28.044 --> 00:35:28.870
All of them.

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:30.880
And fired in two different waves.

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:34.500
The first in 2002 and then again in the
last year.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:38.560
There's no such thing as a safe haven in
this economy.

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:40.730
People will cut jobs as soon as they
decide.

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:44.460
And by the way, you're like a number in
some budgeting exercise.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:46.430
The CFO says, we gotta cuts heads, you
know, 15%.

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:49.910
And there's like all right, take this
whole department out.

00:35:49.910 --> 00:35:52.320
So, just first think I'd think is just,
there's no

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:54.516
such thing as a safe place so if you're
gonna take

00:35:54.516 --> 00:35:56.983
risk, which you are, whether you realize
it or not you

00:35:56.983 --> 00:35:59.420
might as well be in control [LAUGH] of
your own destiny.

00:35:59.420 --> 00:36:02.599
That's the first thing that, that I think
a lot of my classmates got wrong.

00:36:02.599 --> 00:36:04.792
And by the way, when you're a 10 year
Merrill Lynch vet and

00:36:04.792 --> 00:36:07.430
you get whacked in New York City, in the
meltdown of a financial market.

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:09.250
What are you gonna do then?

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:10.390
You're screwed.

00:36:10.390 --> 00:36:12.072
If you're an entrepreneur in Silicon
Valley and it

00:36:12.072 --> 00:36:14.180
doesn't work out, there'll be plenty of
things to do.

00:36:14.180 --> 00:36:15.710
I guarantee you.

00:36:15.710 --> 00:36:18.060
Sec, second thing is I really think

00:36:18.060 --> 00:36:21.765
business schools overteach how rational
organizations are.

00:36:21.765 --> 00:36:25.685
[LAUGH] They basically, basically make up
this thing that

00:36:25.685 --> 00:36:29.255
business, every businesses some DCF model
or some rational

00:36:29.255 --> 00:36:32.335
exercise and then everyone gets around and
they make

00:36:32.335 --> 00:36:35.069
a logic based decision about how, what to
do.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:38.144
When I first got out of the business
school, I

00:36:38.144 --> 00:36:41.430
remember going into meetings and going,
what the fuck just happened?

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:43.910
Like, how did that decision just get made?

00:36:43.910 --> 00:36:48.050
It makes no sense whatsoever, and I
literally was like, this is so weird.

00:36:48.050 --> 00:36:49.988
I, I was like, disoriented for like weeks
and

00:36:49.988 --> 00:36:53.040
just like, I don't understand how anyone's
making their decisions.

00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:54.740
Like, no one's even doing any analysis,
they

00:36:54.740 --> 00:36:56.781
don't understand what they're talking
about and I read

00:36:56.781 --> 00:36:58.182
this book and it was kind of a cliché',

00:36:58.182 --> 00:37:00.250
but it basically said there's two types of
companies.

00:37:00.250 --> 00:37:03.164
There's companies that are idea driven,
where they're

00:37:03.164 --> 00:37:06.018
flat, they're merit based, and people, and
they're

00:37:06.018 --> 00:37:08.574
generally engineering companies in a lot
of ways,

00:37:08.574 --> 00:37:11.080
and they make decisions based on rational
logic.

00:37:11.080 --> 00:37:15.314
And then there's people driven companies
that are driven by politics

00:37:15.314 --> 00:37:19.270
and personal affiliations, and logic has
nothing to do with it.

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:21.423
There may be this kind of pretend that
there's

00:37:21.423 --> 00:37:24.405
a process but basically, there's some
decision that gets made

00:37:24.405 --> 00:37:26.334
based on who knows who or this or that,
and

00:37:26.334 --> 00:37:28.620
all of a sudden it gets announced to the
company.

00:37:28.620 --> 00:37:28.880
And what.

00:37:28.880 --> 00:37:32.950
Generally if you work in those companies,
everyone goes, whoa, what is going on?

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:37.792
And I worked in a lot of people call up
political based organizations and, and

00:37:37.792 --> 00:37:40.026
as a MBA, a business person, I just

00:37:40.026 --> 00:37:44.330
was completely dumbfounded by how
companies were working.

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:48.997
And I, I think that there's, you know,
there's like OB and people talk about

00:37:48.997 --> 00:37:51.085
that, but it really, I think one of

00:37:51.085 --> 00:37:54.151
the challenges about business in general
is [UNKNOWN]

00:37:54.151 --> 00:37:57.007
economics or finances, people presume
logic is

00:37:57.007 --> 00:37:59.863
the basis for how large companies make
decisions

00:37:59.863 --> 00:38:01.395
and all we have to do is look

00:38:01.395 --> 00:38:04.424
at Washington D.C. to realize that's not
true.

00:38:04.424 --> 00:38:07.177
[LAUGH] The last thing I would say is
some,

00:38:07.177 --> 00:38:10.732
for some reason business schools are
prejudice against sales.

00:38:10.732 --> 00:38:13.100
And like they're, I guarantee you when you

00:38:13.100 --> 00:38:16.370
graduate none of your classmates will
become sales people.

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:23.420
And yet John Thompson, John Chambers, Sam
Polusamo, the guys who run

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:24.180
&gt;&gt; Cebo.

00:38:24.180 --> 00:38:24.401
&gt;&gt; Cebo.

00:38:24.401 --> 00:38:26.490
&gt;&gt; John Mortgage.

00:38:26.490 --> 00:38:31.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah, John Mortgage Steve Ballmer ran
sales with Microsoft.

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:34.950
Sales people run the world.

00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:38.199
[LAUGH] You know, like they, they are the
guys that go and like, and

00:38:38.199 --> 00:38:41.760
this, it's very rare that a marketing
person is gonna run Cisco or IBM.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:43.790
You know, it's sales people.

00:38:43.790 --> 00:38:46.040
So, why is it that business schools don't,
don't

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:49.020
embrace sales in sales cultures, and
variable base compensation?

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:54.660
Like, all right, we're gonna pay you 100,
but you can make 300 if you knock it out.

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:58.850
Most MBAs are like, no, I just want a 125
and a little bit of variable.

00:38:58.850 --> 00:38:59.860
But it's like why?

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:03.890
Like, if you're good and you can sell,
then you should go and sell.

00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:06.770
Certainly, whether you're selling to raise
money or selling your product.

00:39:06.770 --> 00:39:08.420
Like, sales is the heart of everything we
do.

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:11.540
And I just wish that someone had said to
me like, go become a sales guy.

00:39:11.540 --> 00:39:13.570
You know, or like learn sales or sales
management.

00:39:13.570 --> 00:39:16.944
Or like, and it's kinda like this dirty
thing, like yeah, I will do

00:39:16.944 --> 00:39:19.272
product management I will do business
strategy

00:39:19.272 --> 00:39:21.260
oh, and then someone's gonna sell it.

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:21.667
Well, who?

00:39:21.667 --> 00:39:22.033
You know?

00:39:22.033 --> 00:39:23.781
[LAUGH] And, and if you're gonna manage
sales

00:39:23.781 --> 00:39:25.490
people it would probably be good to do
that.

00:39:25.490 --> 00:39:27.796
So I, I, I just don't get that part of it.

00:39:27.796 --> 00:39:34.976
MBA's all have the same mental frameworks
rather than make decisions, and

00:39:34.976 --> 00:39:42.398
when you go into a company you have to,
you get almost challenged a little bit.

00:39:42.398 --> 00:39:44.491
Like, you went in with a visual designer
and they're very

00:39:44.491 --> 00:39:46.982
creative and they think in terms of
pictures and, and, and design.

00:39:46.982 --> 00:39:48.785
And then engineers are very logic
architectural driven and

00:39:48.785 --> 00:39:51.120
they won't understand scale and all the
other things.

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:53.052
And when you do a lot of group work at
business

00:39:53.052 --> 00:39:56.543
school, everyone's kinda sharing a common
framework, cuz they're teaching you a,

00:39:56.543 --> 00:39:58.900
a frame, a method of decision making, and
then you get into

00:39:58.900 --> 00:40:01.900
a company, and you realize there's a lot
of this communication problem.

00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:04.166
You're like, how does this person not
understand how they

00:40:04.166 --> 00:40:06.530
think, or it makes no sense what they're
saying to me.

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:07.975
Well, that's because they, they're looking

00:40:07.975 --> 00:40:09.460
at the problem completely differently than
you.

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:12.180
And I would say, the other thing I wish I
had done more of

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:14.523
in business school is do cross-discipline
work,

00:40:14.523 --> 00:40:16.382
like with other people not in my school.

00:40:16.382 --> 00:40:19.634
And I think that D school here's a great
example of that, where you can go

00:40:19.634 --> 00:40:22.141
work with some creative designers and some
engineers,

00:40:22.141 --> 00:40:23.900
and, and you can really realize that to

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:26.409
solve a problem with people from different
disciplines

00:40:26.409 --> 00:40:28.115
requires to be a flexibility in how you

00:40:28.115 --> 00:40:31.510
communicate and how you think about it,
and that was a real shock to me too.

00:40:31.510 --> 00:40:34.040
And you end up like, I have a of couple
people for me who are designers.

00:40:34.040 --> 00:40:35.781
And like whenever I talk to them,

00:40:35.781 --> 00:40:38.453
I realize like, wow, I don't, they're
looking

00:40:38.453 --> 00:40:40.816
at this problem from such a different
vantage

00:40:40.816 --> 00:40:43.380
point than me, but yeah, it's very
valuable.

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:45.560
The way they're thinking and they're
better at it than I am.

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:50.408
So, those are all things that I wish I'd
learned more of I suppose.

00:40:50.408 --> 00:40:56.419
&gt;&gt; [COUGH] so, a, a while ago I had this,
this dream, this fantasy, I like

00:40:56.419 --> 00:41:02.357
gosh I wish I had this magical power that
I could get my company to work.

00:41:02.357 --> 00:41:04.717
I could control the brain of someone I'm
trying

00:41:04.717 --> 00:41:07.490
to get a business development deal with
[LAUGH] you know,

00:41:07.490 --> 00:41:09.791
get inside, get Google to do this, get
Google

00:41:09.791 --> 00:41:12.594
to up you know, change my page rank or
whatever.

00:41:12.594 --> 00:41:16.954
And I'm trying to get around to supporting
Will's

00:41:16.954 --> 00:41:21.499
point that and then I realized you
actually can.

00:41:21.499 --> 00:41:26.520
And that's through this, this persuasion,
this charisma, this

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:32.496
sales technique that I think is the most
important thing CEO's do.

00:41:32.496 --> 00:41:38.033
One way that I got a lot of experience was
in my first company, we were selling

00:41:38.033 --> 00:41:43.530
this software product, $500 software
product over the telephone.

00:41:43.530 --> 00:41:44.913
And there were 3 people in the company.

00:41:44.913 --> 00:41:49.865
And we had just gotten this article in
this magazine, and sure enough, 300

00:41:49.865 --> 00:41:54.630
people called [LAUGH] in the next two days
saying yeah, we wanna get the product.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:57.050
We didn't even have it finished yet, we
were a little bit, jumped the gun.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.333
And so we cr, yeah, we created this
database and

00:41:59.333 --> 00:42:02.630
we started to put everybody's name down
and call 'em back.

00:42:02.630 --> 00:42:04.710
And then more people would call in about
the product.

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:07.147
So in the beginning, out of every 100
calls,

00:42:07.147 --> 00:42:09.643
you know, I would take their information
down, I

00:42:09.643 --> 00:42:11.842
would send them a brochure and I, you
know,

00:42:11.842 --> 00:42:14.650
I'd say okay, I'll follow up or something
like that.

00:42:14.650 --> 00:42:15.769
I basically was closing nothing.

00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:20.892
In 10 weeks later, out of every 100 calls,
I could get 33 of

00:42:20.892 --> 00:42:25.280
them to give me their credit card number
by the end of the call.

00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:26.811
And you just learn to say well look, you
know,

00:42:26.811 --> 00:42:28.461
we have a 30 money back guarantee, so why
don't

00:42:28.461 --> 00:42:29.993
you just give me your credit card and I'll
send

00:42:29.993 --> 00:42:31.558
it to you, then you can just do the
tutorials.

00:42:31.558 --> 00:42:34.083
You don't like it, why do, why do you
wanna spend time reading the brochure?

00:42:34.083 --> 00:42:36.180
Why don't we just have the product in your
hand?

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:38.224
And you can try different techniques and

00:42:38.224 --> 00:42:41.848
different approaches, and learn which ones
work.

00:42:41.848 --> 00:42:44.811
And the other thing I think that happens,
that's

00:42:44.811 --> 00:42:48.098
very powerful is when you ask for
something huge, something

00:42:48.098 --> 00:42:51.126
you think is maybe a little bit
outrageous, and someone

00:42:51.126 --> 00:42:54.362
gives it to you, it's a very eye opening
experience.

00:42:54.362 --> 00:42:57.051
When you say to AOL, you know why don't
you put our

00:42:57.051 --> 00:43:00.907
search results right here and split the
revenue 50 50 with us.

00:43:00.907 --> 00:43:05.036
And when they say okay, you're like, oh my
goodness, [LAUGH] you know.

00:43:05.036 --> 00:43:07.711
There're some other analogies I could give
maybe, probably

00:43:07.711 --> 00:43:10.283
not appropriate in this environment but,
where if you

00:43:10.283 --> 00:43:12.546
go for something you think is maybe
unattainable and

00:43:12.546 --> 00:43:15.450
sometimes they say yes, it's a very
powerful experience.

00:43:15.450 --> 00:43:16.800
And it makes you try more

00:43:19.660 --> 00:43:19.960
often.

00:43:19.960 --> 00:43:20.009
So.

00:43:20.009 --> 00:43:20.180
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:43:20.180 --> 00:43:21.140
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:24.902
So I, I said that business school should
require

00:43:24.902 --> 00:43:29.883
every student before graduating to sell
$10 worth of goods.

00:43:29.883 --> 00:43:30.792
$10.

00:43:31.860 --> 00:43:33.830
I second your point, and I'm gonna move
on.

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:41.116
Selling is underestimated, and it is hard
to get $10 out of a person, believe me.

00:43:41.116 --> 00:43:44.660
It doesn't matter the age, doesn't matter
the size, doesn't.

00:43:44.660 --> 00:43:48.122
So, in our company, at Jooners, we started
as

00:43:48.122 --> 00:43:51.664
a free service, we debugged it and then
realized that

00:43:51.664 --> 00:43:54.103
we have a long way to go for this market

00:43:54.103 --> 00:43:58.100
to realize, and we'd better have some
money coming in.

00:43:58.100 --> 00:44:00.800
So, we turned it into a subscription
service.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:03.470
And, the amount of effort and thinking
that

00:44:03.470 --> 00:44:07.420
went into this exercise, I can write books
about.

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:09.347
But, the net result of it is that we
became

00:44:09.347 --> 00:44:12.580
the, the, the masters of our own universe,
if you will.

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:16.650
It's a very small business, but it is
self-sustaining.

00:44:16.650 --> 00:44:17.070
Right?

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:18.700
So, important stuff: sales.

00:44:18.700 --> 00:44:21.794
I can't, I can't highlight it enough.

00:44:21.794 --> 00:44:23.890
similarly, I thought that in business

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:26.812
school my biggest learning was on
organizational

00:44:26.812 --> 00:44:31.930
behavior, just because I love to learn
more about people and how minds work.

00:44:31.930 --> 00:44:35.780
And accounting, because I knew nothing
about it.

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:38.600
Right, so it, those were the courses that
attracted

00:44:38.600 --> 00:44:40.480
me the most and I learned the most from.

00:44:41.488 --> 00:44:47.470
I, I think there is an over emphasis, at
least in my years.

00:44:47.470 --> 00:44:53.108
But, I, I, I'm sure things have changed on
maximizing

00:44:53.108 --> 00:44:57.961
revenues and profits, as opposed to
maximizing value.

00:44:57.961 --> 00:45:01.292
And that's a critical question that.

00:45:01.292 --> 00:45:06.988
And the reason for it, I think, goes a
little bit to the fact that all of us

00:45:06.988 --> 00:45:14.020
living in U.S capitalist society, and so
for, I, I don't know what the reason is.

00:45:14.020 --> 00:45:15.970
But we always think bigger is better.

00:45:17.330 --> 00:45:21.200
And, I'm not sure if that's the case for
everyone.

00:45:21.200 --> 00:45:27.070
So, understanding on the spectrum of very,
very, very small to very, very,

00:45:27.070 --> 00:45:31.690
very large where your idea, product,
mentality,

00:45:31.690 --> 00:45:35.600
personality, wish for life, you know,
what,whatever.

00:45:35.600 --> 00:45:40.949
Where that fits I think we'll with the
question what am I maximizing?

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:46.420
Certainly you can't create Cisco, just
maximizing value.

00:45:46.420 --> 00:45:49.930
Although some would argue that if value
wasn't maximized

00:45:49.930 --> 00:45:53.260
in some form or fashion, Cisco wouldn't be
created.

00:45:53.260 --> 00:45:56.120
We can have a debate about which one comes
first.

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:58.530
But at the end of the day, the question
that I face

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:02.239
as a CEO in my company right now, is, what
am I maximizing?

00:46:03.290 --> 00:46:03.760
Right?

00:46:03.760 --> 00:46:08.824
Certainly I'm not maximizing number of
people coming to the site and registered

00:46:08.824 --> 00:46:13.450
members because I am putting in front of
them a 1995 barrier, right?

00:46:13.450 --> 00:46:14.140
Subscriptions.

00:46:14.140 --> 00:46:15.200
Give me money.

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:17.020
Otherwise, you can't use the product.

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:18.920
But I'm maximizing.

00:46:18.920 --> 00:46:22.144
The fact that I know the people that I
have are going to

00:46:22.144 --> 00:46:26.047
pay this money, because there is a pain
that I solved for them.

00:46:26.047 --> 00:46:28.490
That's, that's what I'm maximizing right
now.

00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:30.674
So, so, so, on that.

00:46:30.674 --> 00:46:32.430
Another point scale.

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:34.810
When I was at Apple, I was so ignorant.

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:37.530
I would look around and say this is so
stupid.

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:39.210
This is so big.

00:46:39.210 --> 00:46:42.090
And they don't really see the value of my
great ideas.

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:44.300
You know, what, what is going on?

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:45.190
I, I was like you.

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:48.540
I was like I have, I'm an MBA!

00:46:48.540 --> 00:46:48.765
Use me!

00:46:48.765 --> 00:46:49.550
[LAUGH] You know?

00:46:49.550 --> 00:46:50.140
I'm smart.

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:51.240
I can do, you know, etcetera.

00:46:53.560 --> 00:46:56.680
But if you end up in a big company,
please, do

00:46:56.680 --> 00:47:01.540
me a favor and take the time to learn
about scale.

00:47:02.580 --> 00:47:04.220
You know just watch it.

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:09.020
Just watch how organizations are billed,
watch how products are shipped.

00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:10.000
Just watch it.

00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:12.450
Cuz there are a lot of lessons that you'll
learn, that you

00:47:12.450 --> 00:47:15.900
don't know where did, when and where
they'll come handy, but they will.

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:17.660
I guarantee you.

00:47:19.056 --> 00:47:21.610
So scale, it's kind of.

00:47:21.610 --> 00:47:25.340
If you don't understand it, like I was
when I was at Apple, it's easy

00:47:25.340 --> 00:47:29.230
to pooh pooh at and just say, ugh, I'm
just gonna go be an entrepreneur.

00:47:29.230 --> 00:47:31.390
But, if you pay attention and learn from
scale,

00:47:31.390 --> 00:47:33.250
then you can be a better entrepreneur, I
think.

00:47:36.230 --> 00:47:39.950
And I would harp once more, again, learn
where you are weak.

00:47:39.950 --> 00:47:44.580
I think, in business school, because it's
such a selective program at Stanford.

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:47.305
People that come in, we all felt we were
the

00:47:47.305 --> 00:47:51.263
chosen and the school does nothing to
diminish that, right?

00:47:51.263 --> 00:47:55.129
We're, we're continuously pumped, at the
very same time that

00:47:55.129 --> 00:47:58.364
I was going through business school my
husband, my now

00:47:58.364 --> 00:48:02.022
husband, then boyfriend, was going through
a PhD program at

00:48:02.022 --> 00:48:05.120
Stanford and every day he was told how
stupid he is.

00:48:05.120 --> 00:48:05.809
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:48:05.809 --> 00:48:06.670
&gt;&gt; Seriously.

00:48:06.670 --> 00:48:08.580
He was writing a thesis, he was doing
cutting

00:48:08.580 --> 00:48:11.060
edge work, he was doing modelling like you
wouldn't believe.

00:48:12.350 --> 00:48:15.860
And every day, they were telling Murph.s,
yep, you came short, you came short, you.

00:48:15.860 --> 00:48:18.110
And every day, I was, just for sitting in

00:48:18.110 --> 00:48:21.680
these great chairs, I was being told I'm
wonderful.

00:48:21.680 --> 00:48:25.420
And and, and so what happens is that you

00:48:25.420 --> 00:48:28.390
really, I think, gravitate towards what
you're strong at.

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:33.270
As opposed to finding out what you're weak
at.

00:48:33.270 --> 00:48:35.681
And, you know, you may, you may be fine,
you know,

00:48:35.681 --> 00:48:39.250
I'm good at talking, so I'm just gonna
keep on talking.

00:48:39.250 --> 00:48:41.850
I may not be very good at thinking, or
understanding

00:48:41.850 --> 00:48:44.510
people's feelings, so I'm just going let
another person do that.

00:48:44.510 --> 00:48:47.000
That certainly is a strategy, nothing
wrong with that.

00:48:47.000 --> 00:48:48.433
But, if you want to be a

00:48:48.433 --> 00:48:53.280
more holistic manager, and more well
rounded entrepreneur.

00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:56.770
I would definitely pay attention to the
negative space.

00:48:56.770 --> 00:48:58.200
And what it is that you don't do well.

00:48:59.530 --> 00:49:03.055
And why and how can you, how can you beef
it up?

00:49:03.055 --> 00:49:04.435
[SOUND].

00:49:04.435 --> 00:49:04.972
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:49:04.972 --> 00:49:07.810
So, we've got a couple other questions
that we can.

00:49:07.810 --> 00:49:08.870
Throughout here the panel.

00:49:08.870 --> 00:49:14.730
But let's open it up here and see if
there's any questions from the audience.

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:17.920
&gt;&gt; Nobody's mentioned financing on your,
on your own.

00:49:17.920 --> 00:49:20.250
Growing organically.

00:49:20.250 --> 00:49:24.520
I actually have started a number of
businesses one

00:49:24.520 --> 00:49:28.640
of which 30 partners who are each partners
have made

00:49:28.640 --> 00:49:32.290
a million dollars and the company is now
the

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:36.044
biggest company in the United States in
this particular field.

00:49:36.044 --> 00:49:39.900
Can anybody comment on that I never think
on any of it in any business.

00:49:39.900 --> 00:49:45.948
&gt;&gt; Anybody want if not we'll.

00:49:45.948 --> 00:49:51.742
I can so my first one was self financed
with 1500

00:49:51.742 --> 00:49:57.550
dollars we never raised debt or equity
from investors.

00:49:58.720 --> 00:50:02.240
It's quite painful to run a company with
1500

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:06.110
dollars in invested capital it reduces
your flexibility about.

00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:09.160
Having any computers, for example, in the
company.

00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:12.470
So I don't think you have to raise venture

00:50:12.470 --> 00:50:16.710
money, but it's a sort of an optimization
decision.

00:50:16.710 --> 00:50:19.630
The fastest way to get there a lot of ways
is to raise venture money.

00:50:19.630 --> 00:50:22.820
Now I actually haven't raised a lot of

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:27.450
venture money, Direct Hits started with
$1.4 million.

00:50:27.450 --> 00:50:30.682
The next par share was $1 million.

00:50:30.682 --> 00:50:34.340
so, I think you can do just fine with
with.

00:50:34.340 --> 00:50:34.790
See?

00:50:34.790 --> 00:50:37.330
I'm even, Evan drink the Kool-Aid in
Silicon Valley.

00:50:37.330 --> 00:50:38.970
Even small amounts like a million dollars.

00:50:38.970 --> 00:50:40.460
I think you, you can get quite fine with
that.

00:50:42.840 --> 00:50:43.520
&gt;&gt; Any question?

00:50:43.520 --> 00:50:44.560
Right here.

00:50:44.560 --> 00:50:46.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I just, thank you, by the way.

00:50:46.550 --> 00:50:48.860
My name's Erica, and I'm a first year,
PSP.

00:50:48.860 --> 00:50:51.575
And we've had an ethics class and an OB
class,

00:50:51.575 --> 00:50:54.636
and in both of those classes we've been
talking a lot

00:50:54.636 --> 00:50:58.744
about biases recently, and just how we're
inherently biased, kind

00:50:58.744 --> 00:51:03.565
of like self fulfilling nature, and I'm,
I'm constantly struggling with.

00:51:03.565 --> 00:51:07.415
Going with that, and then also looking at
entrepreneurial spirit, because it's

00:51:07.415 --> 00:51:11.615
like, head down, get it done, like, how do
you, there's a little

00:51:11.615 --> 00:51:15.015
bit of talk about in attacking and
reacting to the market, but how

00:51:15.015 --> 00:51:21.315
do you reconcile your own biases with also
just moving straight forward ahead?

00:51:21.315 --> 00:51:23.780
&gt;&gt; By biases, you mean, in terms of.

00:51:23.780 --> 00:51:28.940
Of thinking about what, t, what
consequences might be of a particular

00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:31.600
action or or you're thinking about

00:51:31.600 --> 00:51:34.850
biases against entrepreneurship or for
entrepreneurship?

00:51:34.850 --> 00:51:36.490
&gt;&gt; It was just the, the [INDISCERNIBLE]

00:51:36.490 --> 00:51:39.730
&gt;&gt; How do you deal with your personal,
your, your, your your,

00:51:39.730 --> 00:51:43.958
your personal might call prior, priors in
terms of how the world works?

00:51:43.958 --> 00:51:44.928
Yeah.

00:51:44.928 --> 00:51:47.830
Anybody wanna?

00:51:47.830 --> 00:51:51.180
&gt;&gt; I'll take that, so, I came in with my

00:51:51.180 --> 00:51:54.810
biases about where the direction of the
product should be.

00:51:54.810 --> 00:51:59.070
And, was fortunate that I had team members
that helped me

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:02.900
see how the market was reacting, what was
good and bad, we.

00:52:02.900 --> 00:52:03.890
Switch that.

00:52:03.890 --> 00:52:06.890
But I had very strong values and I went to
school that

00:52:06.890 --> 00:52:10.710
says, you know, if you don't believe it,
nobody else will believe it.

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:15.570
Right, so in that I drove really hard, and
people kept presenting

00:52:15.570 --> 00:52:18.320
me with data of here is how it is not
going to work.

00:52:18.320 --> 00:52:21.710
Other times I haven't done so well So it's
a continuous struggle.

00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:26.950
But, you know, one of the things I do at
the end of everyday.

00:52:26.950 --> 00:52:28.980
I allow ten minutes to just sit.

00:52:30.310 --> 00:52:34.710
And it's amazing the things that occur to
you in the, to me, in those ten minutes.

00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:36.860
I would highly recommend you doing that

00:52:36.860 --> 00:52:40.410
because those types of answers come by
sitting.

00:52:40.410 --> 00:52:42.250
And, and thinking about it.

00:52:42.250 --> 00:52:43.220
You, you, you know what I mean?

00:52:43.220 --> 00:52:45.160
You just need to let them percolate a

00:52:45.160 --> 00:52:48.520
little bit and then the answer will be
self-evident.

00:52:48.520 --> 00:52:49.640
But if you don't believe as

00:52:49.640 --> 00:52:52.300
an entrepreneur, nobody else will believe,
so.

00:52:53.590 --> 00:52:57.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the only thing I've found is
that's really

00:52:57.590 --> 00:53:01.570
been helping me through that is being very
authentic with people.

00:53:01.570 --> 00:53:06.950
And you know you think of entrepreneurs
maybe you know

00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:12.170
there's a fine line between being constant
salesman optimism and being authentic.

00:53:12.170 --> 00:53:13.500
You know and we've all met plenty of
people

00:53:13.500 --> 00:53:15.250
where you're like this guy is such a
snake.

00:53:15.250 --> 00:53:17.140
So you don't believe it.

00:53:17.140 --> 00:53:20.500
So, what I've found that's been really
helpful is having access to people that.

00:53:20.500 --> 00:53:22.760
Or I can just be completely honest with
and let my

00:53:22.760 --> 00:53:27.330
guard down and say here's I'm really
struggling with this and I

00:53:27.330 --> 00:53:31.170
have you know you know obviously if you're
a leader you don't

00:53:31.170 --> 00:53:34.690
wanna panic people by making it seem like
it's hopeless or anything.

00:53:34.690 --> 00:53:37.300
But if you can have access to people.

00:53:37.300 --> 00:53:40.350
You can just generally lay out your
concerns and get feedback.

00:53:40.350 --> 00:53:42.802
You, you really find out that having

00:53:42.802 --> 00:53:46.305
[INAUDIBLE] with the David Hornik's
Conference, the lobby

00:53:46.305 --> 00:53:48.266
in Hawaii and it was just great for

00:53:48.266 --> 00:53:51.560
almost like just the people sharing pain
basically.

00:53:51.560 --> 00:53:52.720
It was willy.

00:53:52.720 --> 00:53:54.270
But in a way, that was very cathartic in

00:53:54.270 --> 00:53:56.920
the sense that you realize like everyone
goes through this.

00:53:56.920 --> 00:54:01.470
Even people who are super confident super
accomplished have gone through very dark

00:54:01.470 --> 00:54:06.085
moments and they have, even you're going
through that and you're concerned, you

00:54:06.085 --> 00:54:10.050
can get recharged by just having that kind
of conversation and being able

00:54:10.050 --> 00:54:13.636
to go back and get stronger to deal with,
you know, your issues.

00:54:13.636 --> 00:54:19.690
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Any other questions?

00:54:19.690 --> 00:54:21.150
Right here.

00:54:21.150 --> 00:54:23.585
&gt;&gt; You mentioned that the things you
learned from [INAUDIBLE].

00:54:23.585 --> 00:54:28.898
[INAUDIBLE] I'd be interested in hearing
some of those

00:54:28.898 --> 00:54:34.817
lessons and takeaways [INAUDIBLE]
aeronautics [INAUDIBLE].

00:54:34.817 --> 00:54:39.067
&gt;&gt; Well, Mike, I already told you you get
a 6% raise.

00:54:39.067 --> 00:54:41.870
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Six and a half.

00:54:41.870 --> 00:54:43.210
&gt;&gt; The average.

00:54:43.210 --> 00:54:43.710
So.

00:54:45.648 --> 00:54:49.430
Mike, so what do you think?

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:51.870
Other than the fact that you learned you
wanted to

00:54:54.070 --> 00:54:54.570
be

00:54:56.330 --> 00:54:57.020
an entrepreneur?

00:54:57.020 --> 00:55:00.260
I mean, Will's point made me think about
why some decisions are made.

00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:03.369
I mean, I was at, let's just say an
unnamed big company where.

00:55:05.690 --> 00:55:07.350
All day long people would go into
meetings.

00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:08.070
There would be no agenda.

00:55:08.070 --> 00:55:09.160
People would talk for an hour.

00:55:09.160 --> 00:55:10.490
There would be no action items taken.

00:55:10.490 --> 00:55:14.160
People would walk out of the meeting and
it's as if the meeting didn't happen.

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:15.390
It's kind of like, why?

00:55:16.588 --> 00:55:19.160
So we don't have very many meetings at my
companies.

00:55:19.160 --> 00:55:25.700
We have a 12 minute weekly all hands
meeting on Monday and.

00:55:25.700 --> 00:55:29.437
We have one on ones you know at 30

00:55:29.437 --> 00:55:35.490
minutes in between back and forth and it's
about it.

00:55:35.490 --> 00:55:41.142
&gt;&gt; So, you gave some examples of the
problems that you saw.

00:55:41.142 --> 00:55:45.880
But was there anything you took away from
that that was valuable.

00:55:47.450 --> 00:55:47.790
Yeah.

00:55:47.790 --> 00:55:49.480
&gt;&gt; Further question.

00:55:49.480 --> 00:55:51.980
&gt;&gt; So one thing to do is watch executives.

00:55:53.630 --> 00:55:55.120
How they run meetings.

00:55:55.120 --> 00:55:56.720
What they do.

00:55:56.720 --> 00:55:59.520
What they're paying attention to.

00:55:59.520 --> 00:56:01.870
And the best way to do that is to get to
know their admin.

00:56:02.890 --> 00:56:06.240
If the admin knows and likes you, you've
got access to the executive.

00:56:06.240 --> 00:56:07.290
So.

00:56:07.290 --> 00:56:12.220
And don't be shy, you know, go and ask
them all your dumb questions.

00:56:12.220 --> 00:56:14.470
How do you run meetings and why?

00:56:14.470 --> 00:56:17.886
What kind of, so those types of access to
people that when

00:56:17.886 --> 00:56:21.980
you come out of that company you will not
have access to.

00:56:21.980 --> 00:56:25.470
The other things I learned was the
decision making process in a big company.

00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:26.070
Company.

00:56:26.070 --> 00:56:30.788
So if you're searching for a partnership
with an AOL, what you wanna do

00:56:30.788 --> 00:56:34.695
is understand how AOL works inside so that
if you have a notion of

00:56:34.695 --> 00:56:38.751
what a VP looks like and what type of
decisions and the size company

00:56:38.751 --> 00:56:41.702
they concern themselves with, what a
director

00:56:41.702 --> 00:56:44.620
looks like, what a manager looks like.

00:56:44.620 --> 00:56:47.280
Then when you're facing them, you speak to
their concerns.

00:56:47.280 --> 00:56:51.430
You're not speaking out of context, if you
will, their context.

00:56:52.620 --> 00:56:55.091
Lots of lessons about, you know,

00:56:55.091 --> 00:56:59.280
effective meetings as well as
non-effective meetings.

00:56:59.280 --> 00:57:03.849
Again, depending the executive, depending
on how the meeting was run.

00:57:05.590 --> 00:57:08.260
And then the whole idea that, you know,
how

00:57:08.260 --> 00:57:10.930
do you make a ship move when there are
thousands

00:57:10.930 --> 00:57:14.100
of people involved and are there any
lessons that can

00:57:14.100 --> 00:57:17.192
be taken from that to apply to a small
company.

00:57:17.192 --> 00:57:21.400
so, yeah, I think it was a really rich
environment.

00:57:21.400 --> 00:57:24.510
Oh, one last thing, lots of training.

00:57:24.510 --> 00:57:27.380
Classes and so forth as they look at large
companies.

00:57:27.380 --> 00:57:29.630
Come to a small company, there is none of
that.

00:57:29.630 --> 00:57:31.760
You have to beg your boss, can I please

00:57:31.760 --> 00:57:36.030
go for this $200, you know,
entrepreneurship conference whatever.

00:57:36.030 --> 00:57:38.670
The answer is, in my company, no.

00:57:38.670 --> 00:57:41.219
And so, so, benefit from those because.

00:57:42.320 --> 00:57:44.010
Larger companies are more accommodating.

00:57:45.240 --> 00:57:47.620
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I started my career at Morgan
Stanley.

00:57:47.620 --> 00:57:50.800
And I went through the analyst training
program there, and I

00:57:50.800 --> 00:57:56.810
learned excellence, work product, and how
to just get stuff done.

00:57:56.810 --> 00:58:01.430
And that was just, you, you're like Friday
4 O'clock, MD calls and says.

00:58:01.430 --> 00:58:03.870
Alright, we're gonna go see a client on
Monday.

00:58:03.870 --> 00:58:07.870
We need to build this gigantic model and
run these 50 different scenarios.

00:58:07.870 --> 00:58:09.550
And by the way, there better not be any
mistakes.

00:58:09.550 --> 00:58:15.010
And have it printed, bound, and at my, at
my home in Westchester by 7 a.m.

00:58:15.010 --> 00:58:16.160
Monday.

00:58:16.160 --> 00:58:20.472
And and I just remember there at Morris
Stanley,

00:58:20.472 --> 00:58:24.960
they taught me once like, hey, the
difference between good

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:29.360
and great is ten minutes [INAUDIBLE] you
do something [UNKNOWN]

00:58:29.360 --> 00:58:33.153
go walk around the block, go get a coffee
[UNKNOWN].

00:58:33.153 --> 00:58:37.362
If you print it out and take it to him and
put it onto his

00:58:37.362 --> 00:58:41.763
desk he will find it for you or she will
find it for you and

00:58:42.910 --> 00:58:44.860
Big companies that are, that maintain a

00:58:44.860 --> 00:58:47.280
culture in excellence in execution can
really teach

00:58:47.280 --> 00:58:49.400
you how to be very personally effective

00:58:49.400 --> 00:58:54.440
and to learn, to functionally execute very
quickly.

00:58:54.440 --> 00:58:58.460
Like build this DCF, now and do this and
do that.

00:58:58.460 --> 00:59:02.080
And so Golden sales that are companies
that are big companies but.

00:59:02.080 --> 00:59:05.100
They're high performance companies where a
lot of good work gets done.

00:59:05.100 --> 00:59:07.380
I remember joking like, those army
commercials we do

00:59:07.380 --> 00:59:09.270
more by six am than most people do all
day.

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:10.800
I used to say that, you know.

00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:12.798
I'd go down to the lobby at Morgan Stanley
and you

00:59:12.798 --> 00:59:14.960
go to work at 5pm and everybody would be
going home.

00:59:14.960 --> 00:59:16.840
You'd be like alright, shift two is about
to start,

00:59:16.840 --> 00:59:18.740
let's go back up and crack some more stuff
out.

00:59:18.740 --> 00:59:20.940
You know, you learn to work.

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:23.130
And that, that I take, I took that away.

00:59:23.130 --> 00:59:24.280
For sure.

00:59:24.280 --> 00:59:29.080
&gt;&gt; So some of you have probably worked
for, or are working for large companies.

00:59:29.080 --> 00:59:31.286
I mean, I think one of the things you
should

00:59:31.286 --> 00:59:34.460
do is take from that that there's things
to learn.

00:59:34.460 --> 00:59:36.530
And it can be important to you as an
entrepreneur.

00:59:37.610 --> 00:59:41.530
15 years ago, certainly 20 years ago, but
even 15 years ago if you asked.

00:59:41.530 --> 00:59:44.601
A venture capitalist in Silicon Valley,
what was the

00:59:44.601 --> 00:59:48.040
right profile for an entrepreneur they
wanted to back.

00:59:48.040 --> 00:59:51.548
It would be, you know, five or ten years
at

00:59:51.548 --> 00:59:57.792
Hewlett-Packard, or some other company to
learn these life lessons.

00:59:57.792 --> 01:00:00.956
If you ask them now, they don't say the
same thing.

01:00:00.956 --> 01:00:02.509
And the reason why.

01:00:02.509 --> 01:00:04.950
&gt;&gt; Is because you have people like these
three people here.

01:00:04.950 --> 01:00:07.704
[COUGH] You can go to work for an
entrepreneurial

01:00:07.704 --> 01:00:11.166
company, and you can learn these lessons
because you

01:00:11.166 --> 01:00:13.353
have a, a set of managers in there who

01:00:13.353 --> 01:00:16.620
really are, really excellent and can teach
you that.

01:00:18.630 --> 01:00:20.480
other, other questions?

01:00:20.480 --> 01:00:21.830
Right here.

01:00:21.830 --> 01:00:25.690
&gt;&gt; I'm an engineer and I don't know if I
would learn sales.

01:00:25.690 --> 01:00:26.610
[INAUDIBLE] Business.

01:00:26.610 --> 01:00:29.400
How am I going to get sales?

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:31.528
&gt;&gt; Well, you could try selling.

01:00:31.528 --> 01:00:32.060
[LAUGH].

01:00:32.060 --> 01:00:32.430
&gt;&gt; I mean, like.

01:00:32.430 --> 01:00:33.900
&gt;&gt; How do you get in there?

01:00:33.900 --> 01:00:38.070
Well, on sales, let me just reinforce what
your panel has said here.

01:00:39.902 --> 01:00:45.726
You think about this, so you, you, you
have a company, and you're

01:00:45.726 --> 01:00:51.260
generating revenue, and it comes time for
a vacation.

01:00:54.476 --> 01:00:55.960
So who do you want to go on vacation?

01:00:55.960 --> 01:00:57.741
Let's say you're going to take a month
vacation.

01:00:57.741 --> 01:01:02.546
Could you get by with the marketing people
gone for a month?

01:01:02.546 --> 01:01:05.460
Could you get by with the finance people
gone for a month?

01:01:05.460 --> 01:01:09.830
The only people you couldn't let go for a
month, are sales and operations.

01:01:09.830 --> 01:01:11.300
You've gotta ship the product, and you
gotta

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:13.310
have someone to sale it, to sell it,
right?

01:01:13.310 --> 01:01:15.650
So it is, it is really important.

01:01:15.650 --> 01:01:18.981
Now, your question is, how do you get a
chance to do that?

01:01:18.981 --> 01:01:20.569
Is that right?

01:01:20.569 --> 01:01:21.029
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:01:21.029 --> 01:01:24.629
How do I get a chance ,but also I never
had an

01:01:24.629 --> 01:01:30.835
opportunity to work at like a
telemarketing firm or something like that.

01:01:30.835 --> 01:01:32.048
&gt;&gt; It's only one kind of sales.

01:01:32.048 --> 01:01:32.579
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:01:32.579 --> 01:01:34.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, right.

01:01:34.860 --> 01:01:36.250
So let's get to the panel here.

01:01:36.250 --> 01:01:40.227
&gt;&gt; All right, I, I, I have the answer for
you [LAUGH] I, I think any company

01:01:40.227 --> 01:01:42.482
you work for as an engineer would welcome

01:01:42.482 --> 01:01:45.730
an opportunity for you to sit on help
desk.

01:01:45.730 --> 01:01:48.110
In my company, I am on help desk at least

01:01:48.110 --> 01:01:51.310
one, once a week, at least for a couple of
hours.

01:01:51.310 --> 01:01:55.450
You'll answer emails and technical
problems or issues

01:01:55.450 --> 01:01:58.140
people are having using your product and
so forth.

01:01:58.140 --> 01:02:01.060
And then through answering those, you will
learn

01:02:01.060 --> 01:02:04.260
how you can shift their perception of the
product.

01:02:04.260 --> 01:02:06.580
That's like step number one in sales.

01:02:06.580 --> 01:02:08.530
You don't have to necessarily get dollars
in

01:02:08.530 --> 01:02:12.360
exchange but you help the company maintain
that customer.

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:12.760
Right?

01:02:12.760 --> 01:02:16.600
So if you're an engineer, best place for
you would be to sit on help desk.

01:02:16.600 --> 01:02:20.524
And do it and offer yourself, and I know
wherever you're working, people

01:02:20.524 --> 01:02:25.050
will jump at the opportunity because, you
know, you have the technical know-how.

01:02:25.050 --> 01:02:26.570
You'll learn a lot that way.

01:02:26.570 --> 01:02:31.550
Then tag along to the sales people as, as
the expert, right?

01:02:31.550 --> 01:02:34.070
They always know how to sell, but they may
not

01:02:34.070 --> 01:02:37.860
have as much in-depth knowledge of the
product as you do.

01:02:37.860 --> 01:02:40.833
So, just ask them you know, hey, take me
with you next time

01:02:40.833 --> 01:02:44.130
you go on a sales call, I promise not to
open my mouth.

01:02:44.130 --> 01:02:48.470
Or, if this is a really hard customer that
has technical problems,

01:02:48.470 --> 01:02:51.800
tell me what they are and I'll come
prepared to answer those.

01:02:51.800 --> 01:02:55.150
I, so I think the first step is just
observation.

01:02:55.150 --> 01:02:57.789
I'll also say that when I was an engineer
I

01:02:57.789 --> 01:03:00.701
wanted more money, and so I looked around
at AMDM,

01:03:00.701 --> 01:03:03.206
and I saw sales people making a lot of
money,

01:03:03.206 --> 01:03:05.820
so I said I want to go be a sale's person.

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:07.190
So I went and applied.

01:03:07.190 --> 01:03:09.260
And they told me, what you're an engineer,
go back to

01:03:09.260 --> 01:03:12.280
your corner, what, what do you mean you
wanna be a salesperson?

01:03:12.280 --> 01:03:13.680
You're an engineer, sit there.

01:03:13.680 --> 01:03:16.512
And that was the reason why I applied to
business school,

01:03:16.512 --> 01:03:19.580
because I thought okay in my limited
worldview, if you wanted

01:03:19.580 --> 01:03:21.999
to sell, you have to go to business school
only to

01:03:21.999 --> 01:03:25.935
learn that there's really no selling
happening in business school, right?

01:03:25.935 --> 01:03:28.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So anyway, they're our avenue.

